he trial and execution of socrates  does that tell us something about just  the general distaste many have to have  their beliefs questioned  well no doubt it does i mean the um  it's it's important i think when you're  thinking thinking about  the ancient world not to imagine these  were the good old days of unfettered  discourse you know you can some people  would say socrates was the first  victim of uh cancel culture he was like  canceled pretty good i i just think the  way i put it is the socratic mindset is  is never going to be  the majority mindset because it's it's  too contrary to how human nature works  people fundamentally crave confirmation  of what they think by others so  somebody's asking questions that are  uncomfortable is never going to be  popular wasn't popular 2500 years ago  isn't popular now uh but i think it's a  very noble and important role and it's  also i'd say the same thing about any  individual the socratic function or the  socratic aspect of your own mind is  never going to be the predominant one  because even in the site even in your  own psyche just like in the social world  it's it's it's never uh the strongest  force you know it's always sort of a  minority position but i think everybody  can use a stronger socratic attitude  within their own self as well as  finding use in the socratic method when  they're dealing with others  the book is the socratic method a  practitioner's handbook we've been  speaking with the book's author ward  farnsworth who's dean of the university  of texas law school i really appreciate  your time today  thank you david great to be here i  appreciate yours
 

